examples.solution_text:
(a) Split $12$ into $3\times4$, then $4$ into $2\times2$. End the branches at primes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node (a) at (0,3) {$84$};
\node (b) at (-1.1,2) {$12$};
\node (c) at (1.1,2) {$7$};
\node (d) at (-1.9,1) {$3$};
\node (e) at (-0.3,1) {$4$};
\node (f) at (-1,0) {$2$};
\node (g) at (0.4,0) {$2$};
\draw (a)--(b) (a)--(c) (b)--(d) (b)--(e) (e)--(f) (e)--(g);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) The prime leaves are $3$, $2$, $2$ and $7$.
$\begin{aligned}84&=2\times2\times3\times7\\&=2^{2}\times3\times7\end{aligned}$

Starting with $8\times15$:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node (a) at (0,3) {$120$};
\node (b) at (-1.6,2) {$8$};
\node (c) at (1.6,2) {$15$};
\node (d) at (-2.4,1) {$2$};
\node (e) at (-0.8,1) {$4$};
\node (f) at (-1.4,0) {$2$};
\node (g) at (-0.2,0) {$2$};
\node (h) at (0.8,1) {$3$};
\node (i) at (2.4,1) {$5$};
\draw (a)--(b) (a)--(c) (b)--(d) (b)--(e) (e)--(f) (e)--(g) (c)--(h) (c)--(i);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$120=(2\times2\times2)\times(3\times5)$.

Starting with $12\times10$:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node (a) at (0,3) {$120$};
\node (b) at (-1.6,2) {$12$};
\node (c) at (1.6,2) {$10$};
\node (d) at (-2.4,1) {$3$};
\node (e) at (-0.8,1) {$4$};
\node (f) at (-1.4,0) {$2$};
\node (g) at (-0.2,0) {$2$};
\node (h) at (0.8,1) {$2$};
\node (i) at (2.4,1) {$5$};
\draw (a)--(b) (a)--(c) (b)--(d) (b)--(e) (e)--(f) (e)--(g) (c)--(h) (c)--(i);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$120=(2\times2\times3)\times(2\times5)$.
Both have three factors of $2$, one $3$ and one $5$.
$120=2^{3}\times3\times5$.
Different starting pairs give the same prime factorisation.

examples.question_text:
The complete factor tree has two leaves, $a$ and $b$, which are different prime numbers.
Which of $35$, $37$, $49$ and $63$ could it factorise? Explain.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\node (n) at (0,1.4) {$?$};
\node (a) at (-1.1,0.4) {$a$};
\node (b) at (1.1,0.4) {$b$};
\draw (n)--(a) (n)--(b);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A student draws this factor tree for $42$.
Explain the mistake and give the correct prime factorisation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
